; Comments here start with a semicolon. Windows' profile API does not treat `#`
; as a comment marker, so a `#key=value` line reaches the component as a key
; literally named `#key` rather than as a disabled setting.

[wambridge]
device=M5
volume=4

; Milliseconds of silence prepended to the stream, 0..10000, default 0.
; This is straight added delay on a path about 6 s long, and it is the only part
; of that delay the project adds to itself. It has been there since the initial
; import with no comment explaining why. Measured at 0 on hardware 2026-08-08:
; startup still reaches WAMBRIDGE PLAYING, so that is 1.5 s back.
;startup_silence=0

; Speaker-facing format: flac, wav, wav24 or mp3, default flac. Thinner is not faster -
; mp3 at 320 kbps measured worse than flac, 16.9 s against 13.4 s, because a
; prebuffer bounded by bytes holds more seconds of a thinner stream. wav pulls
; the same lever the other way: measured 5.68 s against flac's 6.67 s over the
; same source, with half the variation, because its bitrate does not ride the
; music. The cost is depth - flac carries 24 bit here and wav is fixed at 16, and
; its transport passed the checklist on instruments with nobody listening, so it
; stays opt-in. flac is the default and this line only shows the syntax.
;format=flac

; Milliseconds of queue kept on top of foobar's own buffer length, 0..10000,
; default 0. A 2026-08-27 hardware sweep at 1500/1000/500/0 found no starvation;
; at 0 the 2.0 s capacity stayed 1.83-2.00 s full for three minutes, with free
; at 0-167 ms. Higher values add queue and therefore almost the same amount of delay.
;buffer_extra=1000

; Sleep-timer seconds, 0..86400, default 0 (off). This is the one duration
; used both after a stream ends and by Playback -> WAM Bridge -> Start sleep timer.
; Cancel sleep timer sends 0 without changing this preference.
; A fallback, not the mechanism. The M5 does go dark on its own once every
; program talking to it has let go, and the helper now tells the speaker the
; stream is over for exactly that reason. What this firmware has no control
; over is that idle power-down - GetPowerSaving and GetAutoPowerDown do not
; exist - so SetSleepTimer is the only thing it answers if releasing is not
; enough on its own.
; A timer explicitly started from the foobar menu keeps its original deadline across
; helper replacements and foobar restarts and is not cleared by playback startup.
; The automatic after-stop timer is cleared again when the next stream starts - but only
; within the same playback session, because nothing
; records across a foobar restart that one was ever armed. So arming a long
; timer, then setting this back to 0 and restarting foobar, leaves exactly one
; armed timer to fire once. Off by default, because powering the speaker down
; is not this component's decision to make.
;sleep_after_stop=120

; CLOCK line in the foobar console: one a second for the first 240 of each
; stream, then one every thirty seconds for as long as that stream lasts.
; Diagnostic only. The burst runs at 60 lines a minute until it has spent its
; 240 - about 36 KB, once per stream - and after that the rate is 120 lines an
; hour, about 18 KB. The burst is per stream, though, so an hour of skipping
; between tracks pays the per-second rate the whole time; the cheap figure
; describes long uninterrupted listening, not every session. Turn it on before
; reporting anything about pacing, and leave it on to catch a stream that dies
; after ten minutes rather than at startup.
; Reading it: the line comes from the callback foobar uses to ask how much room
; the output has, so it appears only while foobar is working the output loop. A
; gap therefore means "foobar stopped asking", which covers a long pause and a
; full buffer as well as a dead stream - do not read silence as a dropout on its
; own. A format change counts as a new stream too, so a station switching sample
; rate mid-session restarts the burst just like a track change does.
;diagnostics=1

; Highest raw step the FIRST helper of a playback session may start at, 0..30,
; default 3. 0 means no cap. The slider governs everything after the start, so
; this is not a volume limit - it only stops a slider left high after an evening
; of listening from becoming the level the next track opens at, on material
; whose loudness nobody knows yet.
; Measured by ear on the M5 on 15.08.2026, slider mapped onto 0..10: 1 inaudible,
; 2 barely, 3 a little more, 4 clearly louder, 5 distinct, 6 cuts through
; conversation, 7 comfortable. There is no cliff in that curve - the mapping was
; never the problem, the starting point was.
;start_volume_max=3
